From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 20:16:54 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: qcom: Start the PCIe root complex behind a host
 router

A Glymur host router carries the PCIe tunnels of its domain into a
root complex that has no other link. Describe that root complex as a
child node of the router and make it a device only while a tunnel
exists: create it when the connection manager has made the first PCIe
tunnel and the router reports it, and remove it with the last tunnel
and before the router stops.

The connection manager's notifications come under its locks, so they
only count the tunnels. A work item creates and removes the device:
creating it trains the link and enumerates, and removing it unbinds
every driver below. A root complex that does not start is removed and
tried again a few times.

The root complex's driver unbinds from the device when its link is
lost, since only a new start of the controller trains the link again.
A bus notifier sees that, and the work item removes the device and
creates a new one if the tunnel still exists.

The router reports a tunnel in a status register the connection
manager's notification is checked against, because the notification
comes before the tunnel's paths are set up.

Provide the root complex with two resets, since both are router
registers: the hold on the router's side of the pipe interface, and
PERST#, which the router passes down the tunnel and acknowledges.

Report IOMMU DMA protection for the domain when the root complex is
behind a running IOMMU and its root port is external facing; the root
complex's driver does not start otherwise. User space (boltd) then
authorises a device without asking, as on other platforms that confine
tunnelled devices with an IOMMU.

Across system suspend the root complex stays, with a router that is
kept: as a child of the router it is suspended before the router is
halted and resumed after the router has restored the tunnel. The work
item does nothing from the start of the transition to its end, so no
device is added or removed in between; a tunnel that is gone when the
system wakes has its root complex removed then. While the router is
halted the resets fail instead of reaching for it. Hibernation stops
the router, and the root complex with it, as before.

Register use follows the platform's Windows driver.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.2
Signed-off-by: Jim Martin <jimmartin@gmail.com>
